<InsightArticle title="An answer-length assumption can reverse an AI budget decision" description="A frozen AIRH price pair gives different winners for short and long answers. A worked guide to budgeting a range of output lengths without calling it a forecast." date="2026-11-14" type="Explainer" readTime="9 min" snapshot="2026-11-14" standfirst="Budgeting 10,000 jobs from two frozen AIRH price records gives $31.50 and $21.50 when each answer uses 500 tokens. At 8,000 answer tokens, the same comparison becomes $189 and $209, reversing the cheaper candidate.">
<p>Answer length is doing two jobs in that estimate. It changes the amount of text charged for, and it changes how much of the bill is exposed to the output rate. A budget that prints one total without its assumed response length hides both effects. A range can reveal them without pretending to predict how long a real model will answer.</p>
<p>The prices here come from AIRH's frozen 14 November 2026 catalogue, read for this guide on that date. Mistral Large 4 is the first amount in each pair; Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite is the second. We have not sent these jobs to either model. The job count, input length and three output lengths are teaching assumptions, while the rates are historical catalogue records. They are not independently rechecked offers.</p>
<h2>Three possible answer lengths, three different bills</h2>
<p>The example fixes each request at 3,000 input tokens. Input is the text sent to the model, counted in the units its service charges for. It then considers 500, 2,000 and 8,000 output tokens per request. Output is the response side of the calculation. The guide assumes identical counts for both candidates to isolate the arithmetic; a real comparison needs each candidate's actual charged usage.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={['Input tokens per job','Output tokens per job','Jobs','Large: total dollars','Flash-Lite: total dollars']} rows={d.length.map(r=>[r.input,r.output,r.jobs,r.large,r.flashLite])} caption="Scenario table, not measured usage or a forecast. Frozen input/output rates are multiplied by 10,000 hypothetical jobs. No caching, tools, retries, taxes, discounts or additional reasoning charges are included." />
<p>For the first candidate, the input side is $21 across the whole queue: 10,000 multiplied by 3,000, divided by a million, multiplied by $0.70. Its output side is $10.50 at the shortest length, $42 in the middle and $168 at the longest. Adding the same input amount produces the three totals shown in the table.</p>
<p>The second candidate has a $9 input amount across the queue. Its output amounts are $12.50, $50 and $200 at the same assumed lengths. Its cheaper input offsets its dearer output when answers are short. At the longest length, the extra output charge is larger than that input saving. The table therefore changes the ranking as well as the total.</p>
<h2>What would make the middle row a forecast?</h2>
<p>A scenario is a calculation that asks what happens if certain assumptions hold. A forecast adds a claim that some outcome is expected. This table provides the former. We have no measurements establishing that either candidate would average 2,000 output tokens on a particular queue, so the middle row cannot honestly be called the likely bill.</p>
<p>A measured estimate would begin with the intended tasks, the actual prompts and a written definition of an acceptable result. A candidate's output counts would stay attached to whether its answer passed that definition. Otherwise a model that stops early or leaves the task unfinished might appear economical simply because it produces less text. That would turn an incomplete result into an apparent saving.</p>
<p>The sampling choice belongs in the estimate too. A hand-picked set of easy short answers can demonstrate that a workflow runs, but it does not establish the distribution of lengths in the incoming queue. A distribution describes how observations are spread across values. The record needs enough information to show whether long tasks were represented, rather than just printing an average length.</p>
<p>Where the future task mix is unknown, the uncertainty should remain visible. A low and high scenario can be useful planning boundaries without being assigned probabilities. Calling a scenario conservative is a stronger claim: it implies a reason to believe the actual bill will usually stay below it. That reason has not been established by these three invented lengths.</p>
<h2>Long answers deserve a separate explanation</h2>
<p>An unusually long response could reflect a legitimate long task, repeated material or a failure to finish cleanly. This guide does not measure how often those cases occur. For an application trial, keeping the reason beside the token count allows the person responsible for the budget to distinguish useful work from unwanted generation before changing the prompt.</p>
<p>The same point applies to a short response. An economical answer that fully satisfies the task is useful; a truncated one is unresolved. Truncated means cut off before completion. A system that imposes an output limit needs a recorded outcome when that limit is reached. Treating the cut-off response as a successful short answer would give the budget a misleadingly favourable result.</p>
<p>A limit and an expected length are different quantities. The expected length is an estimate of what will be generated. The limit is a rule intended to constrain what can be generated. A useful report names both and explains whether the limit is actually enforced by the application or service. The numbers in this guide specify neither a product feature nor a provider's maximum output capacity.</p>
<p>Changing the output limit can change the task's success rate, so a lower cap needs another quality check. It cannot be treated as a free reduction in cost. A summary that fits a shorter budget may retain the necessary facts; another may omit the very detail the reader asked for. Those are task outcomes, which the rate table cannot decide.</p>
<h2>A queue estimate survives when its assumptions travel with it</h2>
<p>The total amount is easiest to audit when its ingredients remain in the same record. That means the number of jobs, input counts, output counts, rates and exclusions. An account-level bill may also include work outside the sampled application. Reconciling the estimate with that bill requires matching the scope rather than interpreting any difference as an arithmetic error.</p>
<p>Different model tokenisers may count the same text differently. A tokeniser is the procedure that breaks text into model tokens. This example avoids claiming any shared word-to-token conversion: equal counts are an assumption used to demonstrate sensitivity. In an actual trial, the relevant service's usage report provides the counts to price, including any additional charged categories it reports.</p>
<p>Fresh price evidence remains a separate requirement. The frozen file preserves source labels and an import time; the import time does not prove that a person revisited the vendor's page. The <a href="/transparency/#workload-price-guides">transparency receipt</a> lists that limitation, the source file and the reproducible script. No new model measurements or live price checks are claimed.</p>
<p>The table supports a practical question for a budget review: would the choice still make sense if acceptable answers were longer than assumed? Here the answer changes across the stated scenarios. A reviewer can then ask for output measurements before making a decision, with a specific reason for needing them rather than a vague request for more testing.</p>
<p>Where the measured lengths later settle into a narrower range, the estimate can narrow too. The earlier scenarios should remain dated teaching cases rather than being rewritten to look prescient. That preserves what was known at the time and keeps a calculated possibility separate from an observed bill.</p>
<p>A report can retain the long requests separately without discarding them as inconvenient outliers. An outlier is an observation far from the rest of the sample. Whether it represents a fault or genuine demand needs an explanation tied to the task. Removing it solely because it raises the budget would change the question being answered. A second estimate that excludes a documented failure category can sit beside the inclusive estimate, with both scopes clearly named.</p>
<p>For this example, increasing the assumed output by a token on each of the 10,000 jobs adds the output rate divided by a hundred to the queue's dollar bill. That follows directly from 10,000 divided by a million. It gives a reviewer a quick arithmetic check on any revised scenario while leaving the harder question, how many useful tokens the job needs, open for measurement.</p>
</InsightArticle>
